# The data connector — plugging an app into the mesh

The connector exposes a local socket through which an application (on the same
host, or a Docker container mounting the socket) sends and receives **end-to-end**
messages over the mesh. It is the **data plane** (distinct from the web console,
which is the *management* plane).

The node thus becomes the app's network bridge: a chat app, a monitoring agent,
anything that wants to talk to other nodes on the mesh.

## Running it

```bash
python scripts/console_demo.py --connector-port 8790
# -> Data connector: 127.0.0.1:8790   token=<generated token>
```

Or in your own code:

```python
from src.data_connector import DataConnector
conn = DataConnector(node, host="127.0.0.1", port=8790)   # or unix_path="/run/nmesh.sock"
await conn.start()
print(conn.token)
```

## Application sections (app_id)

Several apps can share one node without reading each other's traffic. Every
application message travels in a **section** named by an **app_id**
(`APP_ID_LEN` = 8 bytes, see `src/app_channel.py`). Inside the E2E-encrypted
DATA payload:

```
E2E DATA payload  =  app_id(8)  ‖  app_payload
```

The connector is the **only** place that puts this frame on and takes it off: on
connecting, an app **declares its app_id**; the connector prefixes what it sends
and delivers it **only** the messages of its own section (demultiplexing). A
message with no section header (too short) is **dropped**. Management traffic
(PING/PONG, routing, DHT, handshakes) never uses the DATA plane: it is therefore
structurally outside any section.

Two families of app_id: **built-in** apps (shipped with the node) use a reserved
id derived from the name (`builtin_id`, e.g. `CHAT_APP_ID`) and are not signed;
**deployed** apps are bound to their author's ML-DSA key (`deployed_id`) and
their package is signed (see `Docs/AppSharing/guide`).

## Protocol

Length-prefixed frames (`uint32` big-endian) covering `type(1) | body`.

| Direction | Type | Body | Effect |
|------|------|-------|-------|
| →    | `AUTH` (0x01)       | `app_id(8) + token (utf-8)` | authenticates + declares the section |
| ←    | `AUTH_OK` (0x81)    | —                           | authenticated |
| ←    | `AUTH_FAIL` (0x82)  | —                           | invalid token/section, connection closed |
| →    | `SEND` (0x02)       | `target_id(20) + payload`   | `send_data(target, app_id ‖ payload)` |
| →    | `WHOAMI` (0x03)     | —                           | asks for our node id |
| ←    | `WHOAMI` (0x84)     | `node_id(20)`               | the answer |
| ←    | `RECV` (0x83)       | `source_id(20) + payload`   | an incoming message **from this section** |
| →    | `STORE_GET` (0x04)  | `key(utf-8)`                | reads this section's local drawer |
| →    | `STORE_PUT` (0x05)  | `keylen(2) + key + value`   | writes to the drawer |
| →    | `STORE_DEL` (0x06)  | `key(utf-8)`                | deletes a key |
| →    | `STORE_LIST` (0x07) | —                           | lists the drawer's keys |
| ←    | `STORE_VALUE` (0x85)| `present(1) + value`        | the `STORE_GET` answer |
| ←    | `STORE_OK` (0x86)   | `ok(1)`                     | the `STORE_PUT` / `STORE_DEL` answer |
| ←    | `STORE_KEYS` (0x87) | a JSON array of keys        | the `STORE_LIST` answer |
| →    | `APP_DHT_PUT` (0x08)| `flag(1) + keylen(2) + enc_key + content` | publishes on the app's DHT |
| →    | `APP_DHT_GET` (0x09)| `keylen(2) + dec_key + content_key(20)`   | fetches an entry |
| ←    | `APP_DHT_KEY` (0x88)| `content_key(20)` or empty  | the `APP_DHT_PUT` answer (empty = refused) |
| ←    | `APP_DHT_VALUE`(0x89)| `present(1) + content`     | the `APP_DHT_GET` answer |
| →    | `PSEUDO_PUB` (0x0A)  | `pseudo(utf-8)`             | publishes my pseudo→node id (directory) |
| →    | `PSEUDO_LOOKUP`(0x0B)| `pseudo(utf-8)`             | looks up node ids by pseudo |
| ←    | `PSEUDO_KEY` (0x8A)  | `directory_key(20)` or empty| the `PSEUDO_PUB` answer |
| ←    | `PSEUDO_RESULTS`(0x8B)| JSON `[{id, pseudo}]`      | the `PSEUDO_LOOKUP` answer |
| →    | `AUTH_ASSERT` (0x0C) | `audience(20) + ctx(32) + ttl(4) + purpose(utf-8)` | issues a signed assertion in our name |
| →    | `AUTH_VERIFY` (0x0D) | `flags(1) + ctx(32) + plen(2) + purpose + assertion` | verifies a peer's assertion |
| ←    | `AUTH_ASSERTION`(0x8C)| the assertion, or empty if refused | the `AUTH_ASSERT` answer |
| ←    | `AUTH_PRINCIPAL`(0x8D)| the principal as JSON, or `null` | the `AUTH_VERIFY` answer |

The first frame **must** be `AUTH` (with the app_id). After that the app sends
`SEND` frames and receives `RECV` frames from its section as they come. On the
client side, `ConnectorClient(host, port, token, app_id)` fixes the section;
`from_env` reads `NMESH_APP_ID` (hex) or falls back to the generic section.

The `STORE_*` frames drive the **per-app local store** (the "drawer"): an
encrypted key→value store, isolated per section. The `app_id` **does not appear**
in the frame — the node deduces it from the `AUTH` session, so an app never
touches another's drawer. Details, bounds and solidity: `Docs/AppStorage/guide`.
On the client side: `store_put` / `store_get` / `store_delete` / `store_list`.

The `APP_DHT_*` frames drive the **per-app DHT** (public or private): the app
supplies the content and, for private entries, the encryption key; the node
namespaces by the session's `app_id` and does the DHT crypto. Here too the
`app_id` is absent from the frame. On the client side: `dht_put(content,
enc_key?)` / `dht_get(content_key, dec_key?)`. Details:
`Docs/Architecture/routing.md`.

The `AUTH_*` frames give the app **the node's identity as authentication** (an
SSO): `assert_to(audience, purpose, ctx)` issues an ML-DSA-signed statement —
"this node, in **this** app, to **this** party, for **this** purpose, over
**this** context" — and `verify_assertion(blob, purpose=…, ctx=…)` verifies a
peer's. As with the drawer, the `app_id` **is not in the frame** — the node
deduces it from the `AUTH` session — so an app can never issue for another's
section.

This is **not** a signing oracle: the node never signs bytes chosen by the app,
only a bounded structure in a dedicated domain, with free-form context entering
solely as a 32-byte hash. Without that rule, an app could have a certificate
body signed by the node's key. Details, bounds and threat model:
[`Docs/AppAuth/guide`](../AppAuth/guide).

The `PSEUDO_*` frames drive the **pseudo directory** (network find-by-pseudo):
`publish_pseudo(pseudo)` publishes a signed pseudo→node_id claim (bound to the
session's key), `lookup_pseudo(pseudo)` returns every node id claiming that
pseudo. Namespaced by the session's `app_id`. Details:
`Docs/Architecture/routing.md`.

## Security

- **The token is mandatory** (constant-time comparison) before any action;
  nothing is accepted before `AUTH`.
- Frames are **bounded** in size, the number of clients is **capped**.
- **Loopback by default**; or a **Unix socket** (chmod 0600) for container IPC;
  an `ssl_context` can wrap the TCP listener.
- The connector owns the `node.receive_data()` loop and **demultiplexes** each
  incoming message to the clients of the section concerned (isolation between
  apps; a message with no section is dropped — reject by default).

## Sharing and deploying applications

This connector is the building block: a declared app (manifest + files) launched
by the node plugs into it to talk to the network, on its own section. Sharing
apps over the DHT and their **signed deployment** (app_id + the author's
signature) are described in `Docs/AppSharing/guide`.
